<configs>

	<!--
		=========================================================================
		MAIN MENU HELP PANEL BUILDING BLOCKS
		=========================================================================

		The in-game screen (XUi_InGame) has two levels of navigation: a modlet column
		and a page strip. The main menu panel has one - a single row of tabs, one per
		page, contributed by whoever wants one. It is 450px wide against the in-game
		screen's 1260, which is why it does not try to carry a nav column as well.

		Both templates here mirror their XUi_InGame counterparts in purpose:
		helpmenu_tabbutton is declared once by the window and never by a contributor,
		and helpmenu_page is the whole public API - one element per page.
	-->
	<append xpath="/templates">

		<!--
			Tab button for the strip along the top of the panel.

			A scaled-down copy of vanilla's options_tabbutton + pagingheader_button pair
			(XUi_Menu/templates.xml). Those are sized for the 1800px full-screen options
			pages - font_size 30, collider_padding 20,8 - far too heavy for a 450px side
			panel, and their font size is baked in rather than parameterised, so the
			sizing had to be restated rather than passed in.

			Structure requirements, from XUiC_TabSelectorButton.Init:
			  - the outer rect carries controller="TabSelectorButton"
			  - it needs either an XUiC_SimpleButton child, or ANY descendant view with
			    on_press="true". XUiC_ButtonSelectable is not an XUiC_SimpleButton (it
			    derives from XUiC_Button), so the label below is found by the on_press
			    path, exactly as vanilla's is.

			auto_toggle_selected="false" stops the label toggling its own selected state
			on click - selection is owned by the TabSelector and comes back in via
			selected="{tab_selected}".

			Binding resolution: {tab_selected} and {tab_name_localized} live on the outer
			TabSelectorButton controller, {btn_selected} and {btn_hovered} on the label's
			own ButtonSelectable. Both resolve because BindingItemStandard walks
			Controller.Parent until something answers, skipping plain XUiController nodes.
			The defined() guard on the sprite mirrors vanilla, which guards one level down
			from the button and not at the button itself.

			NO name ATTRIBUTE, deliberately - the same as helpscreen_pagebutton in
			XUi_InGame. The window declares this once under repeat_content and the harness
			names each copy itself: XUiFromXml passes i.ToString() as the view name when it
			expands a repeater. Declaring name="${name}" here would resolve against template
			params that have no such entry.

			SIZED TO ITS CELL rather than to its text. The strip used to be an XUiV_Table,
			which packs children at their natural width, so the button was a 30x20 stub and
			the label grew to fit through overflow="resizefreely". A grid gives every cell a
			fixed 107x32 instead, so the rect takes the cell, the label fills the rect, and
			overflow="ShrinkContent" scales the font down for a caption too long to fit
			rather than letting it run into the neighbouring tab.

			The click target grew with it. collider_padding is gone because the label is now
			cell sized, so on_press covers the whole cell instead of just the glyphs.

			The underline follows suit: anchored to the label edges rather than inset by
			-10/+10 around the text, it now spans the cell. That is what makes a fixed strip
			read as tabs rather than as words with lines under them.

			This template was lbd_tab_button in SphereII Learn By Doing, which used to own
			the whole panel. It moved here with the panel, and the sizing above is the only
			substantive change since.
		-->
		<helpmenu_tabbutton width="107" height="32" font_size="18">
			<rect width="${width}" height="${height}" controller="TabSelectorButton">
				<label name="caption" controller="ButtonSelectable" auto_toggle_selected="false"
				       selected="{tab_selected}" text="{tab_name_localized}"
				       on_press="true" on_hover="true" gamepad_selectable="false" snap="false"
				       depth="6" upper_case="true" font_size="${font_size}"
				       pos="0,0" width="${width}" height="${height}"
				       overflow="ShrinkContent" justify="center" pivot="topleft" max_line_count="1"
				       color="{% btn_selected ? '255,255,255,255' : (btn_hovered ? '255,255,255,255' : '150,150,150,255') }"
				       sound="[paging_click]" sound_play_on_hover="[default_hover]">

					<sprite name="captionBorder" sprite="ui3_border_fade_U" depth="5" type="sliced"
					        fillcenter="{% btn_hovered or (defined('btn_selected') and btn_selected) }"
					        color="{% defined('btn_selected') and btn_selected ? '228,18,21,255' : '255,255,255,255' }"
					        anchor_left="#parent,0,0" anchor_right="#parent,1,0"
					        anchor_bottom="#parent,0,0" anchor_top="#parent,1,0" />
				</label>
			</rect>
		</helpmenu_tabbutton>

		<!--
			A scrolling text page. This is the one element a contributor writes:

			    <helpmenu_page tab_key="yourModMenuOverview" text_key="yourModMenuOverviewBody" />

			tab_key doubles as the tab caption - XUiC_TabSelectorTab.TabKey's setter runs
			it through Localization.Get, and the button opposite reads it back through
			{tab_name_localized}. Literal \n in the CSV becomes a real newline (NGUI's
			ByteReader.ReadCSV does Replace("\\n", "\n") while parsing), and mod
			localization is loaded before the menu is built - ModManager.LoadLocalizations
			runs at startup with _isLoadingInGame false - so menu text resolves.

			SCROLLING is the reason this is a template and not two lines of literal XML.
			The page area is 422x446; a body longer than that used to run straight off the
			bottom of the panel, because XUi rects do not clip. The scroll rig below is the
			same one helpscreen_page uses in XUi_InGame and the same one vanilla's own menu
			windows use - a plain rect carrying on_scroll, holding a defaultscrollbar and a
			scrollview as siblings. XUiV_ScrollView.InitView finds its bar with
			Parent.TryGetChildView<XUiV_ScrollBar>(""), so the two must share a parent.
			defaultscrollbar lives in XUi_Common, so it is available here as well as
			in-game; vanilla's XUi_Menu/windows.xml uses it sixteen times.

			The label is inset 10px from the top for the same reason the in-game one is:
			clippingsoftness maps to NGUI's panel.clipSoftness, which fades by distance
			from the clip edge rather than by scroll position, so text flush against the
			top of the viewport would sit permanently half faded.

			SIZES are literal on purpose. They track the window's tabsContents rect, which
			works out at 430x454 - a 450 wide panel, less 2x10 content padding, and less
			the 46 the tab strip and its rule take off the top. The scrollview leaves 30px
			for the bar and the label another 10 so text never runs under it. Change these
			and windows.xml together or not at all.
		-->
		<helpmenu_page tab_key="" text_key="" font_size="20" width="430" height="454">
			<rect controller="TabSelectorTab" tab_key="${tab_key}">
				<rect name="scrollarea" pos="0,0" width="${width}" height="${height}" depth="5"
				      on_scroll="true" gamepad_selectable="false" snap="false">

					<defaultscrollbar />

					<scrollview pos="0,0" width="${width-30}" clippingsoftness="0,10">
						<label depth="6" name="body" pos="0,-10" width="${width-40}" height="${height}"
						       text_key="${text_key}" font_size="${font_size}" spacing_x="1"
						       color="[lightestGrey]" pivot="topleft" justify="left"
						       overflow="resizeheight" />
					</scrollview>
				</rect>
			</rect>
		</helpmenu_page>

	</append>

</configs>
